<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Basarab I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1351/1352</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Basarab I of Wallachia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Basarab I was the first independent ruler of Wallachia, first recorded in 1324, who united the Romanian lands between the Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathians</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. In November 1330 his forces ambushed and defeated King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-hungary">Charles I of Hungary</a> at Posada, securing Wallachia's independence, and his descendants, the Basarab dynasty, ruled the principality for more than two centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basarab-i.jpg" alt="Basarab I" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: not credited · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basarab_I_of_Wallachia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Many details of Basarab's life are uncertain. A charter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-hungary">Charles I of Hungary</a> of 1332 names his father as Thocomerius, whose social position cannot be determined. According to two popular theories Basarab either came to power between 1304 and 1324 by dethroning or peacefully succeeding Radu Negru, the traditional founder of Wallachia, or in 1310 by succeeding his father. His name is of Turkic origin and suggests Cuman or Pecheneg ancestry, though this has not been proven. Historians dispute whether he was Catholic or Eastern Orthodox: Neagu Djuvara argued from a papal letter of 1327 that he was Catholic, while Matei Cazacu and Dan Mureșan point to sources such as the Illuminated Chronicle, which called him a perfidious schismatic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>A royal charter of 26 July 1324 is the first document to mention him, describing him as voivode of Wallachia under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-hungary">Charles I of Hungary</a>. A charter of 18 June 1325 calls him disloyal to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-crown-of-hungary">Holy Crown of Hungary</a>. He seems to have controlled the Banate of Severin between 1324 and 1330. In 1330 he supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-shishman">Michael Shishman</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, and their armies were routed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-velbazhd">Battle of Velbazhd</a> on 28 July. Charles then invaded Wallachia and took Severin. According to the Illuminated Chronicle, Basarab offered 7,000 marks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and a yearly tribute, which Charles refused.</p><p>Hunger overtook the royal army on its march to Curtea de Argeș, and Charles agreed an armistice. On 9 November 1330, as the army withdrew, the Wallachians ambushed it in a narrow pass of the Southern Carpathians, shooting arrows and throwing rocks from the cliffs. The battle at Posada lasted until 12 November, the royal army was decimated, and Charles narrowly escaped. Basarab then moved his seat to Câmpulung and supported his son-in-law Ivan Alexander in seizing the Bulgarian crown in 1331. According to a charter of his grandson Vladislav I Vlaicu he died in 1351 or 1352.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Tudor Sălăgean writes that the victory at Posada sanctioned Wallachia's independence from the Hungarian crown, and Vlad Georgescu calls Wallachia the first independent Romanian principality. Basarab's descendants ruled it for at least two centuries and included Mircea the Old and Vlad Dracula. From the middle of the 14th century chroniclers used the name Basarab for Wallachia, and the southern part of the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prut">Prut</a> came to be called Basarabia, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Basarab I of Wallachia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He is linked with <b>Basarab I</b>, first mentioned in a Hungarian charter of 1324 as a voivode holding lands south of the Carpathians and paying tribute to Hungary.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-wallachia">Principality of Wallachia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Battle of Posada, fought between 9 and 12 November, Wallachians under the voivode <b>Basarab I</b> ambushed and nearly annihilated the invading army of Charles I of Hungary, ending Hungarian suzerainty in Wallachia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-12">12 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The land later became part of the Principality of Wallachia under <b>Basarab I</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/voivodeship-of-litovoi">Voivodeship of Litovoi</a></li><li><blockquote>Historians see it as one of the polities later united in the Principality of Wallachia by <b>Basarab I</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/voivodeship-of-seneslau">Voivodeship of Seneslau</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Principality of Wallachia, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. Died in Câmpulung, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.42,26.1&amp;year=1342" aria-label="Principality of Wallachia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Basarab I"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4762 3776h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4762 3776h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.42,26.1&amp;year=1342">What was here in 1342? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
